Right fix in another shape, for two defects: upstream's rule, but it
reads the target's roles with has_perm(). It closes every path to an
active account (s3, s5, and the active half of s4). has_perm() is False
for every permission of an inactive account, so a deactivated trainer
(s4) and a deactivated general manager or admin (s6) stay open.

Fixes: s3-reset-password-higher-role, s5-reset-password-general-manager

diff --git a/wger/gym/views/gym.py b/wger/gym/views/gym.py
index b8e8f66..2a0e7f2 100644
--- a/wger/gym/views/gym.py
+++ b/wger/gym/views/gym.py
@@ -266,6 +266,16 @@ def reset_user_password(request, user_pk):
     if request.user.has_perm('gym.manage_gym') and not is_same_gym(request.user, user):
         return HttpResponseForbidden()
 
+    # Resetting a password hands over the account, so the target must hold
+    # fewer gym privileges than the user
+    if not request.user.is_superuser:
+        if user.is_superuser or user.has_perm('gym.manage_gyms'):
+            return HttpResponseForbidden()
+        if not request.user.has_perm('gym.manage_gyms') and (
+            user.has_perm('gym.manage_gym') or user.has_perm('gym.gym_trainer')
+        ):
+            return HttpResponseForbidden()
+
     if request.method != 'POST':
         form = forms.Form()
         form.helper = FormHelper()
